 value chart as follows:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{red}$ | $\\color{red}2$ | **.** |  |  |  |\n\n- The decimal part of the number is *ninety-four thousandths* or $\\color{blue}94$ *thousandths*. The key word here is **thousandths**. So, the rightmost digit ($4$) must sit at the *thousandths* place, as shown below.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{red}$ | $\\color{red}2$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}0$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nNotice that the cell corresponding to the *tenths* place is filled with a zero.\n\nTherefore, *two and ninety-four thousandths* is $2.094$" "We have the following:\n\n- The whole part is *two* $({\\color{red}2})$. We write this in a place value chart as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The decimal part of the number is *ninety-four thousandths* or $\\color{blue}94$ *thousandths*. The key word here is **thousandths**. So, the rightmost digit ($4$) must sit at the *thousandths* place, as shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that the cell corresponding to the *tenths* place is filled with a zero.\n\nTherefore, *two and ninety-four thousandths* is $2.094$"] [:db/cas 17592186138309 :question/worked-solution "We need to convert the given number from standard form to expanded form.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values of $562.01$:\n\n| **hundredts** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $6$ | $2$ | **.** | $0$ | $1$ |\n\n$\\qquad$ $5$ hundreds is $500$, which we can write as $5 \\times 100$\n\n$\\qquad$ $6$ tens is $60$, which we can write as $6 \\times 10$\n\n$\\qquad$ $2$ ones is $2$, which we can write as $2 \\times 1$\n\n$\\qquad$ $0$ tenths is $0$\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ hundredth is $0.01$, which we can write as $1 \\times 0.01$\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n562.01 = 5 \\times 100 + 6 \\times 10 + 2 \\times 1 + 1\\times 0.01\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1 \\times 0.01$." "We need to convert the given number from standard form to expanded form.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values of $562.01$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredts</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n$\\qquad$ $5$ hundreds is $500$, which we can write as $5 \\times 100$\n\n$\\qquad$ $6$ tens is $60$, which we can write as $6 \\times 10$\n\n$\\qquad$ $2$ ones is $2$, which we can write as $2 \\times 1$\n\n$\\qquad$ $0$ tenths is $0$\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ hundredth is $0.01$, which we can write as $1 \\times 0.01$\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n562.01 = 5 \\times 100 + 6 \\times 10 + 2 \\times 1 + 1\\times 0.01\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1 \\times 0.01$."] [:db/cas 17592186138825 :question/problem "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $8$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $2$ |" "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186138827 :question/problem "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $7$ | $6$ |\n| $14$ | $3$ |\n| $20$ | $2$ |" "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186138955 :question/problem "To qualify for the school's soccer team, players must run across the soccer field in less than $12$ seconds. The table below reports the times, in seconds, made by several players.\n\n|  | Time |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Peter | $12.15$ |\n| Sam | $11.1$ |\n| Robert | $13.25$ |\n| Jimmy | $11.83$ |\n\nWhich of the boys qualified for the school's soccer team?" "To qualify for the school's soccer team, players must run across the soccer field in less than $12$ seconds. The table below reports the times, in seconds, made by several players.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Time</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Peter</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$12.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sam</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$11.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Robert</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$13.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Jimmy</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$11.83$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the boys qualified for the school's soccer team?"] [:db/cas 17592186138956 :question/problem "The table below reports the batting average of five girls of a school's softball team.\n\n|  | Average |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Susan | $0.311$ |\n| Mary | $0.283$ |\n| Kathy | $0.317$ |\n| Britney | $0.250$ |\n| Laura | $0.401$ |\n\nWhich player has the highest batting average?" "The table below reports the batting average of five girls of a school's softball team.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Average</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Susan</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$0.311$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Mary</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$0.283$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Kathy</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$0.317$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Britney</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$0.250$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Laura</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$0.401$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich player has the highest batting average?"] [:db/cas 17592186139603 :tutorial/content "When we write down a whole number, the position of each digit has a name.\n\nFor example, let's look at the following three-digit whole number:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}\n$$\n\nFor this number:\n\n- the digit $\\color{purple}2$ is in the **hundreds place**\n- the digit $\\color{Salmon}4$ is in the **tens place**\n- the digit $\\color{SteelBlue}7$ is in the **ones place**\n\nThis tells us that we can rearrange ${\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}$ objects in the following way:\n\n![](images/45/455b92a65a4bdfce5eb1d5c286afac4cc7a9d0710069b838bab73e241fca6e03.png)\n\nIt's more compact to write the place values of a number in a **place value chart**. The place value chart for ${\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}$ is shown below.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{purple}2$ | $\\color{Salmon}4$ | $\\color{SteelBlue}7$ |\n\nThis tells us that ${\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}$ is the same as\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{ {2} \\textrm{ hundreds}}} \\:+\\: {\\color{Salmon}{ {4} \\textrm{ tens}}} \\:+\\: {\\color{SteelBlue}{ {7} \\textrm{ ones}}}.\n$$\n\nHundreds, tens, and ones are known as **place values**." "When we write down a whole number, the position of each digit has a name.\n\nFor example, let's look at the following three-digit whole number:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}\n$$\n\nFor this number:\n\n- the digit $\\color{purple}2$ is in the **hundreds place**\n- the digit $\\color{Salmon}4$ is in the **tens place**\n- the digit $\\color{SteelBlue}7$ is in the **ones place**\n\nThis tells us that we can rearrange ${\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}$ objects in the following way:\n\n![](images/45/455b92a65a4bdfce5eb1d5c286afac4cc7a9d0710069b838bab73e241fca6e03.png)\n\nIt's more compact to write the place values of a number in a **place value chart**. The place value chart for ${\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}$ is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{purple}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{Salmon}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{SteelBlue}7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis tells us that ${\\color{purple}2}{\\color{Salmon}4}{\\color{SteelBlue}7}$ is the same as\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{ {2} \\textrm{ hundreds}}} \\:+\\: {\\color{Salmon}{ {4} \\textrm{ tens}}} \\:+\\: {\\color{SteelBlue}{ {7} \\textrm{ ones}}}.\n$$\n\nHundreds, tens, and ones are known as **place values**."] [:db/cas 17592186139604 :tutorial/content "The place value chart for the four-digit whole number $3,724$ is shown below:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $7$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nThis place value chart has a new **thousands** column.\n\nFor this number:\n\n- the digit $3$ is in the **thousands place**\n- the digit $7$ is in the hundreds place\n- the digit $2$ is in the tens place\n- the digit $4$ is in the ones place\n\nThis tells us that $3,724$ is the same as $3$ thousands, plus $7$ hundreds, plus $2$ tens, plus $4$ ones.\n\nWhen writing down four-digit whole numbers, we separate the thousands and hundreds using a comma \",\" to make it easier to read." "The place value chart for the four-digit whole number $3,724$ is shown below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis place value chart has a new **thousands** column.\n\nFor this number:\n\n- the digit $3$ is in the **thousands place**\n- the digit $7$ is in the hundreds place\n- the digit $2$ is in the tens place\n- the digit $4$ is in the ones place\n\nThis tells us that $3,724$ is the same as $3$ thousands, plus $7$ hundreds, plus $2$ tens, plus $4$ ones.\n\nWhen writing down four-digit whole numbers, we separate the thousands and hundreds using a comma \",\" to make it easier to read."] [:db/cas 17592186139605 :tutorial/content "When we write down a whole number, the place of each digit has a name. This idea is also true with decimals.\n\nFor example, for the number $5.{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}4}$, we can write the digits in a place value chart as follows:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n\nWe say that\n\n- the digit $5$ is in the ones place,\n- the digit $\\color{blue}2$ is in the **tenths place**, and\n- the digit $\\color{red}4$ is in the **hundredths place**.\n\nNotice that there is a column for the decimal point, too. However, the decimal point does *not* correspond to a place value." "When we write down a whole number, the place of each digit has a name. This idea is also true with decimals.\n\nFor example, for the number $5.{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}4}$, we can write the digits in a place value chart as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe say that\n\n- the digit $5$ is in the ones place,\n- the digit $\\color{blue}2$ is in the **tenths place**, and\n- the digit $\\color{red}4$ is in the **hundredths place**.\n\nNotice that there is a column for the decimal point, too. However, the decimal point does *not* correspond to a place value."] [:db/cas 17592186139606 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the number $70.263$. Its place value chart is below:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $0$ | **.** | $2$ | $6$ | $\\color{blue}3$ |\n\nNotice that this table has a new column, which we call the **thousandths** column.\n\nIn this case, we say that $70.263$ has:\n\n- $7$ tens\n- $0$ ones\n- $2$ tenths\n- $6$ hundredths\n- $\\color{blue}3$ **thousandths**" "Let's consider the number $70.263$. Its place value chart is below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that this table has a new column, which we call the **thousandths** column.\n\nIn this case, we say that $70.263$ has:\n\n- $7$ tens\n- $0$ ones\n- $2$ tenths\n- $6$ hundredths\n- $\\color{blue}3$ **thousandths**"] [:db/cas 17592186139607 :tutorial/content "How much bigger is the size of the digit ${\\color{blue}9}$ in $3.{\\color{blue}9}6$ than the size of the digit ${\\color{red}9}$ in $4.3{\\color{red}9}$?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for $3.{\\color{blue}9}6$. We see that the $\\color{blue}9$ is in the tenths place.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}9$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we write the place values for $4.3{\\color{red}9}$. Here, the $\\color{red}9$ is in the hundredths place.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | **.** | $3$ | $\\color{red}9$ |\n\nTo compare the two digits, we move one step to the left in the place value chart. Each step to the left is an *increase* by a factor of $10$:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | **.** | $\\color{blue}9$ | $\\color{red}9$ |\n| ![](images/fd/fdbcaa2c29c81fcdd77d65d1ef3096c1ae46a87d0d8d392e7e6f947a24da86cc.png) |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{blue}9$ in the tenths place is $10$ times greater than the value of the digit $\\color{red}9$ in the hundredths place." "How much bigger is the size of the digit ${\\color{blue}9}$ in $3.{\\color{blue}9}6$ than the size of the digit ${\\color{red}9}$ in $4.3{\\color{red}9}$?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for $3.{\\color{blue}9}6$. We see that the $\\color{blue}9$ is in the tenths place.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we write the place values for $4.3{\\color{red}9}$. Here, the $\\color{red}9$ is in the hundredths place.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo compare the two digits, we move one step to the left in the place value chart. Each step to the left is an *increase* by a factor of $10$:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | **.** | $\\color{blue}9$ | $\\color{red}9$ |\n| ![](images/fd/fdbcaa2c29c81fcdd77d65d1ef3096c1ae46a87d0d8d392e7e6f947a24da86cc.png) |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{blue}9$ in the tenths place is $10$ times greater than the value of the digit $\\color{red}9$ in the hundredths place."] [:db/cas 17592186139608 :tutorial/content "How many times greater is the size of the digit $\\color{blue}2$ in ${\\color{blue}2}3.5$ than the size of the digit $\\color{red}2$ in $98.{\\color{red}2}$?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for ${\\color{blue}{2}}3.5$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $3$ | **.** | $5$ |\n\nThen we write the place values for $98.{\\color{red}{2}}$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $8$ | **.** | $\\color{red}2$ |\n\nNow, let's jump from the tenths place to the tens place. Remember that the decimal does not correspond to a place value, so we jump right over it.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ |  | **.** | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| ![](images/6b/6bbc60ef774908b54aed0086a14932024fc75326befe76fe0e9ac3ab95f4b0e3.png) |  |  |  |\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{10\\times10}_{\\color{black}2\\,\\text{steps}}=100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{blue}2$ in the tens place is $100$ times larger than the value of the digit $\\color{red}2$ in the tenths place." "How many times greater is the size of the digit $\\color{blue}2$ in ${\\color{blue}2}3.5$ than the size of the digit $\\color{red}2$ in $98.{\\color{red}2}$?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for ${\\color{blue}{2}}3.5$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen we write the place values for $98.{\\color{red}{2}}$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's jump from the tenths place to the tens place. Remember that the decimal does not correspond to a place value, so we jump right over it.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ |  | **.** | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| ![](images/6b/6bbc60ef774908b54aed0086a14932024fc75326befe76fe0e9ac3ab95f4b0e3.png) |  |  |  |\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{10\\times10}_{\\color{black}2\\,\\text{steps}}=100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{blue}2$ in the tens place is $100$ times larger than the value of the digit $\\color{red}2$ in the tenths place."] [:db/cas 17592186139609 :tutorial/content "The digit $\\color{red}3$ in $9.15{\\color{red}3}$ is *smaller* than the digit $\\color{blue}3$ in $4.7{\\color{blue}3}$, but by how much?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for $9.15{\\color{red}{3}}$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | **.** | $1$ | $5$ | $\\color{red}3$ |\n\nThen, we write the place values for $4.7{\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | **.** | $7$ | $\\color{blue}3$ | $0$ |\n\nNow, we move from tenths place to thousands place. Each step to the right is a *decrease* by a factor of $10$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | **.** |  | $\\color{blue}3$ | $\\color{red}3$ |\n| ![](images/5e/5ed0900055acc8936fbebe693d32ce69edc66cab25623fe8dd62dfeba8e583c8.png) |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{red}3$ in the thousandths place is $10$ times *smaller* than the value of the digit $\\color{blue}3$ in the hundredths place.\n\nAlternatively, we can also say that the value of the digit $\\color{red}3$ in the thousandths place is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nthe value of the digit $\\color{blue}3$ in the hundredths place." "The digit $\\color{red}3$ in $9.15{\\color{red}3}$ is *smaller* than the digit $\\color{blue}3$ in $4.7{\\color{blue}3}$, but by how much?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for $9.15{\\color{red}{3}}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we write the place values for $4.7{\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we move from tenths place to thousands place. Each step to the right is a *decrease* by a factor of $10$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | **.** |  | $\\color{blue}3$ | $\\color{red}3$ |\n| ![](images/5e/5ed0900055acc8936fbebe693d32ce69edc66cab25623fe8dd62dfeba8e583c8.png) |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{red}3$ in the thousandths place is $10$ times *smaller* than the value of the digit $\\color{blue}3$ in the hundredths place.\n\nAlternatively, we can also say that the value of the digit $\\color{red}3$ in the thousandths place is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nthe value of the digit $\\color{blue}3$ in the hundredths place."] [:db/cas 17592186139611 :tutorial/content "How can we tell if one decimal is smaller than another? For example, is the following statement true?\n\n$$\n2.1 < 2.3\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol \"$<$\" means \"less than.\" So, we want to know if $2.1$ is less than $2.3$.\n\nTo determine whether the statement is true or not, we can use a place value chart. Let's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | **.** | $\\color{red}1$ |\n| $2$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}3$ |\n\nWe now compare the place values of both numbers, going *from left to right*. We stop when we find two digits that are different.\n\n- Looking at the digits in the ones place, we see that they are the same for both numbers ($2$).\n- Moving on to the tenths place, we see that the digit ($\\color{red}1$) is *less than* the digit ($\\color{blue}3$). Using the \"less than\" symbol, we can write, ${\\color{red}1} < {\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTherefore, the statement $2.{\\color{red}1} < 2.{\\color{blue}3}$ is true." "How can we tell if one decimal is smaller than another? For example, is the following statement true?\n\n$$\n2.1 < 2.3\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol \"$<$\" means \"less than.\" So, we want to know if $2.1$ is less than $2.3$.\n\nTo determine whether the statement is true or not, we can use a place value chart. Let's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now compare the place values of both numbers, going *from left to right*. We stop when we find two digits that are different.\n\n- Looking at the digits in the ones place, we see that they are the same for both numbers ($2$).\n- Moving on to the tenths place, we see that the digit ($\\color{red}1$) is *less than* the digit ($\\color{blue}3$). Using the \"less than\" symbol, we can write, ${\\color{red}1} < {\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTherefore, the statement $2.{\\color{red}1} < 2.{\\color{blue}3}$ is true."] [:db/cas 17592186139612 :tutorial/content "Is the following statement true?\n\n$$\n3.12 > 3.09\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol \"$>$\" means \"greater than.\" In words, we want to know if $3.12$ is greater than $3.09$.\n\nOnce again, we can use a place value chart. Let's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | **.** | $\\color{red}0$ | $9$ |\n\nWe now compare the place values of both numbers, going *from left to right*.\n\n- Looking at the digits in the ones place, we see that they are the same for both numbers ($3$).\n- Moving on to the tenths place, we see that digit $\\color{blue}1$ is *greater than* the digit ${\\color{red}0}$. Using the \"greater than\" symbol, we can write ${\\color{blue}1} > {\\color{red}0}$.\n\nTherefore, the statement $3.{\\color{blue}1}2 > 3.{\\color{red}0}9$ is true." "Is the following statement true?\n\n$$\n3.12 > 3.09\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol \"$>$\" means \"greater than.\" In words, we want to know if $3.12$ is greater than $3.09$.\n\nOnce again, we can use a place value chart. Let's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe now compare the place values of both numbers, going *from left to right*.\n\n- Looking at the digits in the ones place, we see that they are the same for both numbers ($3$).\n- Moving on to the tenths place, we see that digit $\\color{blue}1$ is *greater than* the digit ${\\color{red}0}$. Using the \"greater than\" symbol, we can write ${\\color{blue}1} > {\\color{red}0}$.\n\nTherefore, the statement $3.{\\color{blue}1}2 > 3.{\\color{red}0}9$ is true."] [:db/cas 17592186139619 :tutorial/content "The **expanded form** of a decimal number expresses the number as a sum of each digit multiplied by its place value.\n\nTo illustrate, let's write the decimal number $85.92$ in expanded form. We start by writing down the place values:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $5$ | **.** | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nTo write the number in expanded form, we multiply each digit by its place value and add them up. So in our case, this gives:\n\n$$\n(8\\times10) + (5\\times 1) + \\left(9\\times \\dfrac{1}{10}\\right) + \\left(2\\times\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right)\n$$" "The **expanded form** of a decimal number expresses the number as a sum of each digit multiplied by its place value.\n\nTo illustrate, let's write the decimal number $85.92$ in expanded form. We start by writing down the place values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo write the number in expanded form, we multiply each digit by its place value and add them up. So in our case, this gives:\n\n$$\n(8\\times10) + (5\\times 1) + \\left(9\\times \\dfrac{1}{10}\\right) + \\left(2\\times\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139620 :tutorial/content "When zeros appear in the standard form of a decimal, they do not appear in the expanded form.\n\nFor example, $1.{\\color{blue}0}5$ has a $\\color{blue}0$ in the tenths place.\n\nTo convert this into expanded form, we start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}0$ | $5$ |\n\nWe have:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ one is $1$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\color{blue}0$ tenths is $\\color{blue}0$\n\n$\\qquad$ $5$ hundredths is $0.05$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n1.05 = 1 + {\\color{blue}0} + 0.05\n$$\n\nBut adding zero doesn't change a number, so we can remove it. Hence, our final answer is:\n\n$$\n1.05 = 1 + 0.05\n$$" "When zeros appear in the standard form of a decimal, they do not appear in the expanded form.\n\nFor example, $1.{\\color{blue}0}5$ has a $\\color{blue}0$ in the tenths place.\n\nTo convert this into expanded form, we start by writing down the place values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe have:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ one is $1$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\color{blue}0$ tenths is $\\color{blue}0$\n\n$\\qquad$ $5$ hundredths is $0.05$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n1.05 = 1 + {\\color{blue}0} + 0.05\n$$\n\nBut adding zero doesn't change a number, so we can remove it. Hence, our final answer is:\n\n$$\n1.05 = 1 + 0.05\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139621 :tutorial/content "A decimal number is in expanded form when it is written as the sum of place values. For instance, the following number is in expanded form:\n\n$$\n3 + 0.5\n$$\n\nSuppose that we need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. To do this, notice that:\n\n$\\qquad$ $3$ is $3$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$ $0.5$ is $5$ tenths.\n\nWe can put this information into a place value chart. By doing this, we get:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | **.** | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, the standard form representation of our decimal is $3.5$." "A decimal number is in expanded form when it is written as the sum of place values. For instance, the following number is in expanded form:\n\n$$\n3 + 0.5\n$$\n\nSuppose that we need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. To do this, notice that:\n\n$\\qquad$ $3$ is $3$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$ $0.5$ is $5$ tenths.\n\nWe can put this information into a place value chart. By doing this, we get:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the standard form representation of our decimal is $3.5$."] [:db/cas 17592186139622 :tutorial/content "In the expanded form\n\n$$\n(2\\times 1) + \\left(5\\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\\right)+\\left(7\\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\right)\n$$\n\nthere is no place value for the hundredths place. We have:\n\n$\\qquad$ $2\\times 1$ is $2$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nis $5$ tenths.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n7\\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\n$$\n\nis $7$ thousandths.\n\nSince there are no hundredths in the given expression, we say we have $\\color{blue}0$ hundredths.\n\nPutting this into the place value chart, we get:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | **.** | $5$ | $\\color{blue}0$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, the given expanded form is equivalent to $2.5{\\color{blue}0}7$." "In the expanded form\n\n$$\n(2\\times 1) + \\left(5\\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\\right)+\\left(7\\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\right)\n$$\n\nthere is no place value for the hundredths place. We have:\n\n$\\qquad$ $2\\times 1$ is $2$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nis $5$ tenths.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n7\\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\n$$\n\nis $7$ thousandths.\n\nSince there are no hundredths in the given expression, we say we have $\\color{blue}0$ hundredths.\n\nPutting this into the place value chart, we get:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the given expanded form is equivalent to $2.5{\\color{blue}0}7$."] [:db/cas 17592186139623 :tutorial/content "We can use place value charts to divide decimals by $10$. For instance, to find the value of\n\n$$\n28 \\div 10\n$$\n\nwe perform the following steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $28$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $8$ | **.** |  |\n\n**Step 2.** Move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $2$ | **.** | $8$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28\\div 10 = 2.8 \\,\n$$" "We can use place value charts to divide decimals by $10$. For instance, to find the value of\n\n$$\n28 \\div 10\n$$\n\nwe perform the following steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $28$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28\\div 10 = 2.8 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139624 :tutorial/content "When dividing by a power of ten, we shift the digits in the place value chart to the *right* by the number of zeros in the power of ten.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the value of $47.2 \\div 100$.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $47.2$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $7$ | **.** | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** Determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $\\color{blue}2$ places:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **.** | $4$ | $7$ | $2$ |\n\n**Step 3.** Put zero in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point. We can also put a zero in the ones place if it is empty.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | **.** | $4$ | $7$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n47.2\\div 100 = 0.472 \\,\n$$" "When dividing by a power of ten, we shift the digits in the place value chart to the *right* by the number of zeros in the power of ten.\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the value of $47.2 \\div 100$.\n\n**Step 1.** Write down the place value chart for $47.2$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $\\color{blue}2$ places:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 3.** Put zero in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point. We can also put a zero in the ones place if it is empty.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousandths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n47.2\\div 100 = 0.472 \\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139639 :tutorial/content "We can use place values to express decimals in words in a systematic way.\n\nFor instance, let's figure out how to express $0.4$ in words. We start with its place value chart.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}0$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nTo write a decimal (less than $1$) in words, we perform the following steps:\n\n- First, since the whole number part is $\\color{red}0$, we ignore it.\n- Next, we write the decimal part as a whole number. Here, the decimal part is ${\\color{blue}4}$, so we write *four*.\n- Finally, we write the place name of the rightmost digit. Here, the rightmost digit is ${\\color{blue}4}$, which has the place name *tenths*.\n\nPutting these all together, we conclude that $0.4$ expressed in words is *four tenths*." "We can use place values to express decimals in words in a systematic way.\n\nFor instance, let's figure out how to express $0.4$ in words. We start with its place value chart.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo write a decimal (less than $1$) in words, we perform the following steps:\n\n- First, since the whole number part is $\\color{red}0$, we ignore it.\n- Next, we write the decimal part as a whole number. Here, the decimal part is ${\\color{blue}4}$, so we write *four*.\n- Finally, we write the place name of the rightmost digit. Here, the rightmost digit is ${\\color{blue}4}$, which has the place name *tenths*.\n\nPutting these all together, we conclude that $0.4$ expressed in words is *four tenths*."] [:db/cas 17592186139640 :tutorial/content "When we want to express a decimal containing a whole number part in words, we write the whole number first. Then, we proceed as usual with the decimal part.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's express the decimal $9.1$ in words. The place value chart for this number is given below.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}9$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}1$ |\n\nTo write this decimal in words, we perform the following steps:\n\n- First, we write the whole number part. Here, the whole part of the number is ${\\color{red}9}$, so we first write *nine*.\n- Then, we write *and* to represent the decimal point.\n- Finally, we write the decimal part as before. The decimal part of the number is $\\color{blue}1$ (*one*), and the rightmost digit sits at the *tenths* place. So, we write it as *one tenth*.\n\nTherefore, $9.1$ expressed in words is *nine and one tenth*." "When we want to express a decimal containing a whole number part in words, we write the whole number first. Then, we proceed as usual with the decimal part.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's express the decimal $9.1$ in words. The place value chart for this number is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo write this decimal in words, we perform the following steps:\n\n- First, we write the whole number part. Here, the whole part of the number is ${\\color{red}9}$, so we first write *nine*.\n- Then, we write *and* to represent the decimal point.\n- Finally, we write the decimal part as before. The decimal part of the number is $\\color{blue}1$ (*one*), and the rightmost digit sits at the *tenths* place. So, we write it as *one tenth*.\n\nTherefore, $9.1$ expressed in words is *nine and one tenth*."] [:db/cas 17592186139683 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to convert numbers from standard form to expanded form. We'll now learn how to reverse the process.\n\nLet's convert the following expanded form to standard form:\n\n$$\n500 +40 +3\n$$\n\nFirst, we write each number as a place value:\n\n- $500$ is $\\color{blue}5$ hundreds\n- $40$ is $\\color{red}4$ tens\n- $3$ is $\\color{magenta}3$ ones\n\nPutting these into a place value chart, we get the following:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}5$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{magenta}3$ |\n\nTherefore, the given number (in standard form) is $543$." "We've seen how to convert numbers from standard form to expanded form. We'll now learn how to reverse the process.\n\nLet's convert the following expanded form to standard form:\n\n$$\n500 +40 +3\n$$\n\nFirst, we write each number as a place value:\n\n- $500$ is $\\color{blue}5$ hundreds\n- $40$ is $\\color{red}4$ tens\n- $3$ is $\\color{magenta}3$ ones\n\nPutting these into a place value chart, we get the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{magenta}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the given number (in standard form) is $543$."] [:db/cas 17592186139684 :tutorial/content "When working with large numbers, writing them as a sum of place values is sometimes helpful.\n\nLet's write the following number as a sum of hundreds, tens, and ones:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{6}}{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| ${\\color{purple}{6}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ | ${\\color{red}{4}}$ |\n\nNext, we interpret each place value as a number:\n\n- ${\\color{purple}{6}}$ hundreds is ${\\color{purple}{6}}00$\n- ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ tens is ${\\color{blue}{2}}0$\n- ${\\color{red}{4}}$ ones is ${\\color{red}{4}}$\n\nTherefore, we can write $624$ as a sum of hundreds, tens, and ones as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{6}}{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}{4}} = {\\color{purple}{6}}00 + {\\color{blue}{2}}0 + {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The way we usually write numbers is called **standard form**. In the example above, $624$ is a number written in standard form.\n- When a number is written as a sum of its place values, we say it's in **expanded form.** In the example above, $600+20+4$ is a number written in expanded form." "When working with large numbers, writing them as a sum of place values is sometimes helpful.\n\nLet's write the following number as a sum of hundreds, tens, and ones:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{6}}{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing down the place values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{purple}{6}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{2}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{4}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we interpret each place value as a number:\n\n- ${\\color{purple}{6}}$ hundreds is ${\\color{purple}{6}}00$\n- ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ tens is ${\\color{blue}{2}}0$\n- ${\\color{red}{4}}$ ones is ${\\color{red}{4}}$\n\nTherefore, we can write $624$ as a sum of hundreds, tens, and ones as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{6}}{\\color{blue}{2}}{\\color{red}{4}} = {\\color{purple}{6}}00 + {\\color{blue}{2}}0 + {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The way we usually write numbers is called **standard form**. In the example above, $624$ is a number written in standard form.\n- When a number is written as a sum of its place values, we say it's in **expanded form.** In the example above, $600+20+4$ is a number written in expanded form."] [:db/cas 17592186139685 :tutorial/content "Let's write the following number in expanded form:\n\n$$\n8,{\\color{blue}0}94\n$$\n\nNotice that this number has a ${\\color{blue}0}$ in the hundreds place.\n\nTo write this number in expanded form, we write down the place values.\n\n| **Thousands** | **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $\\color{blue}0$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nNext, we interpret each place value as a number:\n\n- $8$ thousands is $8,000$\n- $\\color{blue}0$ hundreds is ${\\color{blue}0}$\n- $9$ tens is $90$\n- $4$ ones is $4$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n8,094 = 8,000 + {\\color{blue}0} + 90 + 4\n$$\n\nSince adding $\\color{blue}0$ to a number doesn't change it, we can remove this zero. So, our final answer is\n\n$$\n8,094 = 8,000 + 90 + 4\n$$" "Let's write the following number in expanded form:\n\n$$\n8,{\\color{blue}0}94\n$$\n\nNotice that this number has a ${\\color{blue}0}$ in the hundreds place.\n\nTo write this number in expanded form, we write down the place values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we interpret each place value as a number:\n\n- $8$ thousands is $8,000$\n- $\\color{blue}0$ hundreds is ${\\color{blue}0}$\n- $9$ tens is $90$\n- $4$ ones is $4$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n8,094 = 8,000 + {\\color{blue}0} + 90 + 4\n$$\n\nSince adding $\\color{blue}0$ to a number doesn't change it, we can remove this zero. So, our final answer is\n\n$$\n8,094 = 8,000 + 90 + 4\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139713 :tutorial/content "We need to be extra careful when converting from one unit of area to another.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a square has an area of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$. Let's use unit arguments to express this area in square millimeters.\n\nWe start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from $\\textrm{cm}^2$ to $\\textrm{mm}^2$, we start with the conversion between $\\textrm{cm}$ and $\\textrm{mm}$. According to our table, there are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** This is *not* the conversion factor we need. If we multiply $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by this conversion factor, we will *not* get an equivalent area!\n\nThe problem with using this conversion factor is that it does not contain area units. However, we can convert this into area units by squaring it and applying the rules of exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{10mm}{cm}\\right)^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is now the correct conversion factor. It tells us that there are $100$ square millimeters per square centimeter.\n\nNow, multiplying $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ is equivalent to $200\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$." "We need to be extra careful when converting from one unit of area to another.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a square has an area of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$. Let's use unit arguments to express this area in square millimeters.\n\nWe start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we're converting from $\\textrm{cm}^2$ to $\\textrm{mm}^2$, we start with the conversion between $\\textrm{cm}$ and $\\textrm{mm}$. According to our table, there are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** This is *not* the conversion factor we need. If we multiply $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by this conversion factor, we will *not* get an equivalent area!\n\nThe problem with using this conversion factor is that it does not contain area units. However, we can convert this into area units by squaring it and applying the rules of exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{10mm}{cm}\\right)^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is now the correct conversion factor. It tells us that there are $100$ square millimeters per square centimeter.\n\nNow, multiplying $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ is equivalent to $200\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$."] [:db/cas 17592186139728 :tutorial/content "We already know how to find ${\\color{red}{39}}\\times1{\\color{blue}{0}}$. We join together ${\\color{red}{39}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{0}}$:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{39}}\\times1{\\color{blue}{0}} = {\\color{red}{39}}{\\color{blue}{0}}\n$$\n\nTo see why this works, we can use a place value chart.\n\n**Step 1**: First, we write down the place value chart for $39$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n\n**Step 2.** Since we're multiplying by $10$, we move all digits to the *left* by $1$ place and place a zero in the empty cell on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $3$ | $9$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ |\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n39\\times 10 = 390\n$$\n\nThis process works because a digit in a given place value is $10$ times smaller than the same digit one place value to the left. Therefore, to multiply a number by $10$, we move all of the digits by one place to the left." "We already know how to find ${\\color{red}{39}}\\times1{\\color{blue}{0}}$. We join together ${\\color{red}{39}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{0}}$:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{39}}\\times1{\\color{blue}{0}} = {\\color{red}{39}}{\\color{blue}{0}}\n$$\n\nTo see why this works, we can use a place value chart.\n\n**Step 1**: First, we write down the place value chart for $39$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Since we're multiplying by $10$, we move all digits to the *left* by $1$ place and place a zero in the empty cell on the right.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n39\\times 10 = 390\n$$\n\nThis process works because a digit in a given place value is $10$ times smaller than the same digit one place value to the left. Therefore, to multiply a number by $10$, we move all of the digits by one place to the left."] [:db/cas 17592186139729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to find $3\\times100$ using a place value chart. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We write down the place value chart for $3$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\n**Step 2.** Since $1{\\color{blue}{00}}$ contains a block of $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, we move all of the digits to the left by $\\color{blue}2$ spaces and place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ |\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\times 100 = 300\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying a number by $100$ is the same as multiplying by $10$ twice, which means that we move to the left by two places." "Suppose that we want to find $3\\times100$ using a place value chart. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We write down the place value chart for $3$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Since $1{\\color{blue}{00}}$ contains a block of $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, we move all of the digits to the left by $\\color{blue}2$ spaces and place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\times 100 = 300\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying a number by $100$ is the same as multiplying by $10$ twice, which means that we move to the left by two places."] [:db/cas 17592186139730 :tutorial/content "A **power of $\\mathbf{10}$** is a whole number where the leading digit is one, and the remaining digits are all zero.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are all powers of $10{:}$\n\n$$\n10, \\qquad 100, \\qquad 1,000, \\qquad 10,000, \\qquad 100,000\n$$\n\nUsing a place value chart, we can multiply a number by any power of ten.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to compute $7\\times 1,000$. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We write down the place value chart for $7$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $7$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We count the zeros in the power of $10$. Our power of ten is $1,{\\color{blue}{000}}$, which contains a block of $\\color{blue}3$ zeros. Therefore, we move all digits by $\\color{blue}3$ places to the left and place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $7$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7\\times 1,000 = 7,000\n$$" "A **power of $\\mathbf{10}$** is a whole number where the leading digit is one, and the remaining digits are all zero.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are all powers of $10{:}$\n\n$$\n10, \\qquad 100, \\qquad 1,000, \\qquad 10,000, \\qquad 100,000\n$$\n\nUsing a place value chart, we can multiply a number by any power of ten.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to compute $7\\times 1,000$. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We write down the place value chart for $7$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** We count the zeros in the power of $10$. Our power of ten is $1,{\\color{blue}{000}}$, which contains a block of $\\color{blue}3$ zeros. Therefore, we move all digits by $\\color{blue}3$ places to the left and place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7\\times 1,000 = 7,000\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186139732 :tutorial/content "If a decimal has an extra zero at the end, we can remove this extra zero without changing the number.\n\nFor example, to find a decimal that's the same as $5.90$, we simply remove the zero at the end:\n\n$$\n5.9\\cancel{0} = 5.9\n$$\n\nBecause $5.90$ and $5.9$ represent the same decimal number, we say that they are **equivalent decimals**.\n\nWhy does this work? Let's look at the place value chart for $5.90$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | **.** | $9$ | $0$ |\n\nWe can see that there is a zero in the hundredths column, and there are no smaller place values (for example, there are no thousandths). So, we can remove the hundredths column:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | **.** | $9$ |" "If a decimal has an extra zero at the end, we can remove this extra zero without changing the number.\n\nFor example, to find a decimal that's the same as $5.90$, we simply remove the zero at the end:\n\n$$\n5.9\\cancel{0} = 5.9\n$$\n\nBecause $5.90$ and $5.9$ represent the same decimal number, we say that they are **equivalent decimals**.\n\nWhy does this work? Let's look at the place value chart for $5.90$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundredths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can see that there is a zero in the hundredths column, and there are no smaller place values (for example, there are no thousandths). So, we can remove the hundredths column:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tenths</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>.</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186141904 :answer/value "| Pattern A | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ |" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern 