d-solution "First, we find the number of whole apples given to each child.\n\n- $4$ halves is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle 4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2\n$$ apples.\n- $3$ halves is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{2} = 1\\, \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$ apples.\n- $1$ half is equivalent to $1 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ of an apple.\n\nWe organize these results in the table:\n\n| Child | Half-Apples | Number of Apples |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Margaret | $4$ | $2$ |\n| Daniel | $3$ | $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| Rose | $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n\nSo, we have the following dataset for the number of apples given to each child:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad 1\\, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad 2\n$$\n\nThis dataset has the following line plot:\n\n![](images/ca/ca5fe6bedfd44c1a0f3e3a7bc2ae858d922c74d1ecc815ee0ac7a17f8ab4b4e7.png)" "First, we find the number of whole apples given to each child.\n\n- $4$ halves is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle 4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2\n$$ apples.\n- $3$ halves is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{2} = 1\\, \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$ apples.\n- $1$ half is equivalent to $1 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ of an apple.\n\nWe organize these results in the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Child</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Half-Apples</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Apples</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Margaret</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Daniel</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Rose</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td> </tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we have the following dataset for the number of apples given to each child:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad 1\\, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad 2\n$$\n\nThis dataset has the following line plot:\n\n![](images/ca/ca5fe6bedfd44c1a0f3e3a7bc2ae858d922c74d1ecc815ee0ac7a17f8ab4b4e7.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186142680 :question/problem "The following table reports the typing speed, in words per minute, of ten students after an online typing test.\n\nMake a line plot to represent the given table.\n\n| Name | Typing speed |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Elizabeth | $40$ |\n| Simon | $40$ |\n| Joseph | $40$ |\n| Emma | $41$ |\n| Edward | $42$ |\n| Lilly | $42$ |\n| Paul | $42$ |\n| Fiona | $43$ |\n| Gaby | $43$ |\n| John | $44$ |" "The following table reports the typing speed, in words per minute, of ten students after an online typing test.\n\nMake a line plot to represent the given table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Typing speed</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Elizabeth</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Simon</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Joseph</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Emma</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Edward</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Lilly</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Paul</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$42$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Fiona</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$43$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">Gaby</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$43$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">John</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$44$</td> </tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186142688 :question/worked-solution "We have $4$ points with the following coordinates:\n\n| $x$-coordinate | $y$-coordinate |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $150$ |\n| $2$ | $300$ |\n| $3$ | $450$ |\n\nPlotting these, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/a8/a8a401f480c8f24c7c8c78bebd05c86f64233c88676af6fc5ce04036dff8bdf6.png)" "We have $4$ points with the following coordinates:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$-coordinate</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$-coordinate</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$150$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$300$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$450$</td> </tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting these, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/a8/a8a401f480c8f24c7c8c78bebd05c86f64233c88676af6fc5ce04036dff8bdf6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186142696 :question/problem "Consider patterns A and B, shown in the table below.\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $2$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: subtract $6$ | $36$ | $30$ | $24$ | $18$ | $12$ |\n\nWhat is missing in the following sentence?\n\n- Each term in pattern A is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern B." "Consider patterns A and B, shown in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: subtract $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: subtract $6$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat is missing in the following sentence?\n\n- Each term in pattern A is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern B."] [:db/cas 17592186142697 :question/problem "The leftmost column of the table below defines two patterns, Pattern A and Pattern B. You're also given a single number that appears in each pattern.\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $5$ |  | $16$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: subtract $15$ | $63$ |  |  |  |\n\nBy finding the missing numbers in each pattern, complete the following sentence:\n\n- Each term in pattern B is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern A." "The leftmost column of the table below defines two patterns, Pattern A and Pattern B. You're also given a single number that appears in each pattern.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: subtract $5$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: subtract $15$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$63$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBy finding the missing numbers in each pattern, complete the following sentence:\n\n- Each term in pattern B is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern A."] [:db/cas 17592186142697 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"subtract $5$\", and the second number is $16$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/dc/dc077278e9fc5cd56591c7d0f16758e13c6b7296dfe49b38520cc2411a9185ed.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"subtract $15$\", and the first number is $63$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/09/0962caa71326f7bb9d32ea8bcd487a8be1b8a2c56a33e4b8d2fb08f7debf33fd.png)\n\nSo we can fill in the table:\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $5$ | $21$ | $16$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: subtract $15$ | $63$ | $48$ | $33$ | $18$ |\n\nNote that we can multiply each term in pattern A by $3$ to get the corresponding term in pattern B:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21 \\cdot 3 &= 63 \\\\\n16 \\cdot 3 &= 48 \\\\\n11 \\cdot 3 &= 33 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 3 &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern B is **$3$ times larger** than the corresponding term in pattern A." "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"subtract $5$\", and the second number is $16$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/dc/dc077278e9fc5cd56591c7d0f16758e13c6b7296dfe49b38520cc2411a9185ed.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"subtract $15$\", and the first number is $63$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/09/0962caa71326f7bb9d32ea8bcd487a8be1b8a2c56a33e4b8d2fb08f7debf33fd.png)\n\nSo we can fill in the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: subtract $5$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: subtract $15$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$63$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$48$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$33$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote that we can multiply each term in pattern A by $3$ to get the corresponding term in pattern B:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21 \\cdot 3 &= 63 \\\\\n16 \\cdot 3 &= 48 \\\\\n11 \\cdot 3 &= 33 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 3 &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern B is **$3$ times larger** than the corresponding term in pattern A."] [:db/cas 17592186142698 :question/problem "The leftmost column of the table below defines two patterns, Pattern A and Pattern B. You're also given the first number in each pattern.\n\n| Pattern A: add $3$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $18$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n\nBy finding the missing numbers in each pattern, complete the following sentence:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern B." "The leftmost column of the table below defines two patterns, Pattern A and Pattern B. You're also given the first number in each pattern.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $3$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: add $18$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBy finding the missing numbers in each pattern, complete the following sentence:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern B."] [:db/cas 17592186142698 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"add $3$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/fb/fbaa7ceb815c926a39dadbded56310fa4dae533391b621826082b838055d513d.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"add $18$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/2b/2b41bc6d8aae1a484c01f995a69c5da094dc3564cbb878a90ce22ec2bfa5dba1.png)\n\nSo we can fill in the table:\n\n| Pattern A: add $3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $18$ | $0$ | $18$ | $36$ | $54$ |\n\nNotice that we can divide each term in pattern B by $6$ to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 \\div 6 &= 0 \\\\\n18 \\div 6 &= 3 \\\\\n36 \\div 6 &= 6 \\\\\n54 \\div 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is **$6$ times smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern B." "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"add $3$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/fb/fbaa7ceb815c926a39dadbded56310fa4dae533391b621826082b838055d513d.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"add $18$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/2b/2b41bc6d8aae1a484c01f995a69c5da094dc3564cbb878a90ce22ec2bfa5dba1.png)\n\nSo we can fill in the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $3$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: add $18$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$54$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that we can divide each term in pattern B by $6$ to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 \\div 6 &= 0 \\\\\n18 \\div 6 &= 3 \\\\\n36 \\div 6 &= 6 \\\\\n54 \\div 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is **$6$ times smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern B."] [:db/cas 17592186142699 :question/problem "Stephanie wants to find the relationship between the terms in patterns A and B below. She wants to do this without calculating all of the values.\n\n| Pattern A: add $7$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $21$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n\nShe writes the following down in her notebook.\n\n- *Both patterns start from zero*\n- *The rule for pattern A is \"add $7$\".*\n- *The rule for pattern B is \"add $21$\".*\n- *Therefore, the terms in pattern B are $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{0000000000000}}}$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern A.*\n\nWhat should be inserted into the blank space to make Stephanie's answer correct?" "Stephanie wants to find the relationship between the terms in patterns A and B below. She wants to do this without calculating all of the values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $7$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: add $21$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nShe writes the following down in her notebook.\n\n- *Both patterns start from zero*\n- *The rule for pattern A is \"add $7$\".*\n- *The rule for pattern B is \"add $21$\".*\n- *Therefore, the terms in pattern B are $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{0000000000000}}}$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern A.*\n\nWhat should be inserted into the blank space to make Stephanie's answer correct?"] [:db/cas 17592186142700 :question/problem "Find the numbers for the patterns shown in the table below and plot the corresponding graph.\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: multiply by $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |" "Find the numbers for the patterns shown in the table below and plot the corresponding graph.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: multiply by $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186142700 :question/worked-solution "The rule of pattern A is \"add $2$\", and the first number is $1$. So, we have the following pattern:\n\n![](images/11/11004332da0b3d1ff785c451b2d05962835cfa1251ba4eabe7583e5b18d861b9.png)\n\nThe rule of pattern B is \"multiply by $2$\", and the first number is $1$. So, we have the following pattern:\n\n![](images/69/69483006f1634704954bf3aa5d55b2b1a7aef6690686550d42c40b6e5b915d48.png)\n\nLet's fill in the table:\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: multiply by $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nWe choose the horizontal axis ($x$-axis) to correspond to pattern A and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) to correspond to pattern B. We add one more row to the table with ordered pairs corresponding to each point.\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: multiply by $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| Ordered pair | $(1,1)$ | $(3,2)$ | $(5,4)$ | $(7,8)$ |\n\nPlotting each ordered pair on the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/e6/e6b3f5ac6218b16db0f72df02f6215d5d42254e31caa935d4cecf91c45b78b10.png)" "The rule of pattern A is \"add $2$\", and the first number is $1$. So, we have the following pattern:\n\n![](images/11/11004332da0b3d1ff785c451b2d05962835cfa1251ba4eabe7583e5b18d861b9.png)\n\nThe rule of pattern B is \"multiply by $2$\", and the first number is $1$. So, we have the following pattern:\n\n![](images/69/69483006f1634704954bf3aa5d55b2b1a7aef6690686550d42c40b6e5b915d48.png)\n\nLet's fill in the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: multiply by $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe choose the horizontal axis ($x$-axis) to correspond to pattern A and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) to correspond to pattern B. We add one more row to the table with ordered pairs corresponding to each point.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: multiply by $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Ordered pair</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,1)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(3,2)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(5,4)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(7,8)$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting each ordered pair on the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/e6/e6b3f5ac6218b16db0f72df02f6215d5d42254e31caa935d4cecf91c45b78b10.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186142701 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n\n$$\n(1,10),\\, (2,20),\\, (3,40),\\, (4,80)\n$$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n| Ordered pair | $(1,10)$ | $(2,20)$ | $(3,40)$ | $(4,80)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern A | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| Pattern B | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $80$ |\n\nLooking at pattern B only, we see that the rule is \"multiply by $2$\":\n\n![](images/45/45c1e55b3e08c0b756f8644e9045592f9366b7d072eb7aa6730dba6b064f71ea.png)" "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n\n$$\n(1,10),\\, (2,20),\\, (3,40),\\, (4,80)\n$$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Ordered pair</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,10)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,20)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(3,40)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(4,80)$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at pattern B only, we see that the rule is \"multiply by $2$\":\n\n![](images/45/45c1e55b3e08c0b756f8644e9045592f9366b7d072eb7aa6730dba6b064f71ea.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186142702 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n$(1,10),\\, (3,8),\\, (5,6),\\, (7,4),\\, (9,2)$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n| Ordered pair | $(1,10)$ | $(3,8)$ | $(5,6)$ | $(7,4)$ | $(9,2)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern A | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| Pattern B | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nRemoving the first row, we get:\n\n| Pattern A | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |" "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n$(1,10),\\, (3,8),\\, (5,6),\\, (7,4),\\, (9,2)$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Ordered pair</th>\n<td>$(1,10)$</td> <td>$(3,8)$</td> <td>$(5,6)$</td> <td>$(7,4)$</td><td>$(9,2)$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRemoving the first row, we get:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186142703 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\, (3,1),\\, (4,2),\\, (5,3)\n$$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n| Ordered pair | $(2,0)$ | $(3,1)$ | $(4,2)$ | $(5,3)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern A | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| Pattern B | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nComparing the terms in both patterns, we note that we can add $2$ to each term of pattern B to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n1 + 2 &= 3 \\\\\n2 + 2 &= 4 \\\\\n3 + 2 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n$\\qquad$ Each term in pattern B is **$2$ smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern A." "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\, (3,1),\\, (4,2),\\, (5,3)\n$$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Ordered pair</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,0)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(3,1)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(4,2)$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(5,3)$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nComparing the terms in both patterns, we note that we can add $2$ to each term of pattern B to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n1 + 2 &= 3 \\\\\n2 + 2 &= 4 \\\\\n3 + 2 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n$\\qquad$ Each term in pattern B is **$2$ smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern A."] [:db/cas 17592186143259 :question/problem "The elevation of a location is measured relative to height above sea level, in meters. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n| An elevation of $-4m$ means $4$ meters below sea level. | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: |\n| If elevation moves from $-2m$ to $3m$, it increases by $5$ meters. | {{field-2}} |\n| An elevation of $0m$ means \"at sea level.\" | {{field-3}} |" "The elevation of a location is measured relative to height above sea level, in meters. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">An elevation of $-4m$ means $4$ meters below sea level.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">If elevation moves from $-2m$ to $3m$, it increases by $5$ m