 B: add $6$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHe writes the following down in his notebook.\n\n- *Both patterns start from zero*\n- *The rule for pattern A is \"add $2$\".*\n- *The rule for pattern B is \"add $6$\".*\n- *Therefore, the terms in pattern B are $\\underset{―}{\\phantom{ {}^{0000000000000}}}$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern A.*\n\nWhat should be inserted into the blank space to make Gabriel's answer correct?"] [:db/cas 17592186143486 :question/problem "Find the numbers for the patterns shown in the table below and select the corresponding graph.\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $5$ | $15$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: divide by $3$ | $27$ |  |  |  |" "Find the numbers for the patterns shown in the table below and select the corresponding graph.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: subtract $5$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: divide by $3$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186143988 :tutorial/content "We can use graphs to analyze relationships between different sets of real-world data.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the coach of a soccer club keeps track of the number of goals scored by the best players, as well as the number of games played by each player. The table below represents some of this data.\n\n|  | Games | Total goals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| **David** | $3$ | $2$ |\n| **Sam** | $2$ | $1$ |\n| **Owen** | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe can draw a coordinate plane to represent the same data as the table. There are two variables, the number of goals and the number of games, and each axis will correspond to one variable.\n\nWe choose the horizontal $\\color{red}x$-axis to represent the number of games and the vertical $\\color{blue}y$-axis to represent the total number of goals.\n\nThis gives us $3$ points, one for each player, with the following coordinates:\n\n|  | $x$-coordinate | $y$-coordinate |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| **David** | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |\n| **Sam** | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{blue}1$ |\n| **Owen** | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nPlotting this on the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/89/89d512c0599807961b964ef8372e3b7b4172c68c021ef3c393f8be0a669e362f.png)" "We can use graphs to analyze relationships between different sets of real-world data.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the coach of a soccer club keeps track of the number of goals scored by the best players, as well as the number of games played by each player. The table below represents some of this data.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Games</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Total goals</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\"><strong>David</strong></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\"><strong>Sam</strong></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\"><strong>Owen</strong></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td> </tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can draw a coordinate plane to represent the same data as the table. There are two variables, the number of goals and the number of games, and each axis will correspond to one variable.\n\nWe choose the horizontal $\\color{red}x$-axis to represent the number of games and the vertical $\\color{blue}y$-axis to represent the total number of goals.\n\nThis gives us $3$ points, one for each player, with the following coordinates:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$-coordinate</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$-coordinate</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\"><strong>David</strong></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\"><strong>Sam</strong></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td> </tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\"><strong>Owen</strong></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td> </tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting this on the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/89/89d512c0599807961b964ef8372e3b7b4172c68c021ef3c393f8be0a669e362f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186143992 :tutorial/content "We can compare two patterns by comparing the corresponding terms.\n\nFor example, consider patterns A and B, shown in the table below.\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $\\color{blue}0$ | $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $\\color{blue}8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $4$ | $\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}12$ | $\\color{red}16$ |\n\nNote that we can multiply each term in pattern A by $2$ to get the corresponding term in pattern B:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 \\cdot 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2 \\cdot 2 &= 4 \\\\\n4 \\cdot 2 &= 8 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= 12 \\\\\n8 \\cdot 2 &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can state the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"Each term in pattern B is **$2$ times larger** than the corresponding term in pattern A.\"*" "We can compare two patterns by comparing the corresponding terms.\n\nFor example, consider patterns A and B, shown in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $2$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: add $4$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}8$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote that we can multiply each term in pattern A by $2$ to get the corresponding term in pattern B:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 \\cdot 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2 \\cdot 2 &= 4 \\\\\n4 \\cdot 2 &= 8 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= 12 \\\\\n8 \\cdot 2 &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can state the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"Each term in pattern B is **$2$ times larger** than the corresponding term in pattern A.\"*"] [:db/cas 17592186143994 :tutorial/content "We can plot the relationship between two patterns, such as those given in the table below, in a coordinate plane.\n\n| Pattern A: add $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $5$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n\nTo do this, we choose the horizontal axis ($x$-axis) to correspond to pattern A and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) to correspond to pattern B.\n\nWe add one more row to the table that contains the ordered pairs corresponding to each point. Here, the $x$ and $y$-coordinates are the terms from patterns A and B, respectively.\n\n| Pattern A: add $1$ | $\\color{blue}0$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{blue}3$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $5$ | $\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}10$ | $\\color{red}15$ | $\\color{red}20$ |\n| Ordered pair | $({\\color{blue}0},{\\color{red}0})$ | $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}5})$ | $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}10})$ | $({\\color{blue}3},{\\color{red}15})$ | $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}20})$ |\n\nPlotting each ordered pair in the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/4e/4ecb261ce4661dea8484bdc62ccb520701f9b72b3063bf954faf07eddf7a2cc0.png)" "We can plot the relationship between two patterns, such as those given in the table below, in a coordinate plane.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $1$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: add $5$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo do this, we choose the horizontal axis ($x$-axis) to correspond to pattern A and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) to correspond to pattern B.\n\nWe add one more row to the table that contains the ordered pairs corresponding to each point. Here, the $x$ and $y$-coordinates are the terms from patterns A and B, respectively.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern A: add $1$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pattern B: add $5$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}15$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td></tr>\n<tr data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Ordered pair</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$({\\color{blue}0},{\\color{red}0})$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}5})$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}10})$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$({\\color{blue}3},{\\color{red}15})$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}20})$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nPlotting each ordered pair in the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/4e/4ecb261ce4661dea8484bdc62ccb520701f9b72b3063bf954faf07eddf7a2cc0.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186144015 :tutorial/content "**Box models** allow us to solve division problems by breaking them into smaller, easier steps.\n\nIn this approach, we repeatedly subtract easy multiples of the divisor from the dividend. Each multiple we subtract gives a partial quotient, and the sum of all partial quotients is the final quotient.\n\nThis strategy is called **partial quotients,** and the box model helps us visually track the steps.\n\nLet's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n36 \\div 2\n$$\n\nThe box model for this division problem looks as follows:\n\n|  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$$6$ |  |\n\nWe can solve this division problem by following four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Pick a multiple of $2$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than $36$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n2 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 20\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}10}$ above the box and subtract $2 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $36$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$$6$$-$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}6$ |  |\n\n**Step 2**: Repeat the process. We write $\\color{red}16$ in a new box to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$$6$$-$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}6$ |\n\n**Step 3**: Pick a multiple of $2$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than ${\\color{red}16}$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n2 \\times {\\color{blue}8} = 16\n$$\n\nWe write $\\color{blue}8$ above the box and subtract $2 \\times {\\color{blue}8}$ from $\\color{red}16$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$$6$$-$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}6$$-$$1$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\n**Step 4**: To find the quotient of $36 \\div 2$, we add the numbers at the very top:\n\n$$\n10+8=18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n36 \\div 2 = 18\n$$" "**Box models** allow us to solve division problems by breaking them into smaller, easier steps.\n\nIn this approach, we repeatedly subtract easy multiples of the divisor from the dividend. Each multiple we subtract gives a partial quotient, and the sum of all partial quotients is the final quotient.\n\nThis strategy is called **partial quotients,** and the box model helps us visually track the steps.\n\nLet's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n36 \\div 2\n$$\n\nThe box model for this division problem looks as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can solve this division problem by following four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Pick a multiple of $2$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than $36$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n2 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 20\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}10}$ above the box and subtract $2 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $36$ inside the box.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rul