></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\n**Step 4**: To find the quotient of $630 \\div 3$, we add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n200+10=210\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n630 \\div 3 = 210\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example with a remainder."] [:db/cas 17592186147266 :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $10$ | $6$ |\n\nThe equation that represents the relationship between $y$ and $x$ is $y = x - \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}.$ What is the missing number?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td></tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe equation that represents the relationship between $y$ and $x$ is $y = x - \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}.$ What is the missing number?"] [:db/cas 17592186147266 :question/worked-solution "We notice that, for every $x$ value, we subtract $4$ to get the corresponding $y$ value.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $4 - {\\color{blue}4} = 0$ |\n| $6$ | $6 - {\\color{blue}4}=2$ |\n| $8$ | $8-{\\color{blue}4}=4$ |\n| $10$ | $10-{\\color{blue}4}=6$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = x - {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $4$." "We notice that, for every $x$ value, we subtract $4$ to get the corresponding $y$ value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4 - {\\color{blue}4} = 0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6 - {\\color{blue}4}=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8-{\\color{blue}4}=4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-{\\color{blue}4}=6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = x - {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $4$."] [:db/cas 17592186147267 :question/problem "| $a$ | $b$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10$ | $2$ |\n| $20$ | $4$ |\n| $30$ | $6$ |\n| $40$ | $8$ |\n\nThe equation that represents the relationship between $b$ and $a$ is $b = \\dfrac{a}{ \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}.$ Find the missing number." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$a$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$b$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe equation that represents the relationship between $b$ and $a$ is $b = \\dfrac{a}{ \\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}.$ Find the missing number."] [:db/cas 17592186147267 :question/worked-solution "We notice that, for every $a$-value, we divide by $\\color{blue}5$ to get the corresponding $b$-value.\n\n| $a$ | $b$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10$ | $\\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5} = 2$ |\n| $20$ | $\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}5}=4$ |\n| $30$ | $\\dfrac{30}{\\color{blue}5}=6$ |\n| $40$ | $\\dfrac{40}{\\color{blue}5}=8$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nb = \\dfrac{a}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $5$." "We notice that, for every $a$-value, we divide by $\\color{blue}5$ to get the corresponding $b$-value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$a$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$b$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5} = 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}5}=4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{30}{\\color{blue}5}=6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{40}{\\color{blue}5}=8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nb = \\dfrac{a}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $5$."] [:db/cas 17592186147268 :question/problem "| $p$ | $q$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ | $12$ |\n| $4$ | $16$ |\n|  | $20$ |\n\nThe table represents the equation $q = 4p$. From **top to bottom**, what are the missing numbers?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td></tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#C0C1C2\"></td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table represents the equation $q = 4p$. From **top to bottom**, what are the missing numbers?"] [:db/cas 17592186147268 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the missing value of $q$, we substitute $p=2$ into the relationship $q = 4p{:}$\n\n$$\nq = 4 \\times 2 = {\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nTo find the second missing value, we substitute $q=20$ into the relationship $q = 4p{:}$\n$20 = 4p$\n\nNow, we solve this equation by dividing both sides by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 20 & \\displaystyle  = 4 p \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{20}{4}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4 p}{4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle 5 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{4} p}{\\cancel{4}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}5} & \\displaystyle  = p\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $5$.\n\n| $p$ | $q$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ |\n| $3$ | $12$ |\n| $4$ | $16$ |\n| ${\\color{red}{5}}$ | $20$ |" "To calculate the missing value of $q$, we substitute $p=2$ into the relationship $q = 4p{:}$\n\n$$\nq = 4 \\times 2 = {\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nTo find the second missing value, we substitute $q=20$ into the relationship $q = 4p{:}$\n$20 = 4p$\n\nNow, we solve this equation by dividing both sides by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 20 & \\displaystyle  = 4 p \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{20}{4}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4 p}{4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle 5 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{4} p}{\\cancel{4}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}5} & \\displaystyle  = p\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $5$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{8}}$</td>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{5}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186147269 :question/problem "| $z$ | $t$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | $1$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ |\n| $9$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n\nWhat equation represents the relationship between $z$ and $t$?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat equation represents the relationship between $z$ and $t$?"] [:db/cas 17592186147269 :question/worked-solution "Note that, for every $z$-value, we divide by $\\color{blue}3$ to get the corresponding $t$-value.\n\n| $z$ | $t$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | $3 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 1$ |\n| $6$ | $6 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 2$ |\n| $9$ | $9 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 3$ |\n| $12$ | $12 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 4$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{z}{3}\n$$" "Note that, for every $z$-value, we divide by $\\color{blue}3$ to get the corresponding $t$-value.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{z}{3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186147305 :question/problem "Heather surveyed the amount of milk consumed per week by several households. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n| Liters of Milk | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $6$ | $10$ |\n| $8$ | $15$ |\n\nHow many respondents said they consumed $6$ liters of milk per week?" "Heather surveyed the amount of milk consumed per week by several households. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Liters of Milk</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHow many respondents said they consumed $6$ liters of milk per week?"] [:db/cas 17592186147305 :question/worked-solution "First, let's highlight the relevant row in the table.\n\n| Liters of Milk | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{blue}10$ |\n| $8$ | $15$ |\n\nFrom the table, we can see that $\\color{blue}10$ households consume $\\color{red}6$ liters of milk per week." "First, let's highlight the relevant row in the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Liters of Milk</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#FDEDEC\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the table, we can see that $\\color{blue}10$ households consume $\\color{red}6$ liters of milk per week."] [:db/cas 17592186147306 :question/problem "Mary asked some family members how many glasses of water they drink daily. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n| Glasses of Water | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-3$ | $4$ |\n| $4-6$ | $8$ |\n| $7-9$ | $5$ |\n| $10-12$ | $1$ |\n\nHow many family members did Mary consult?" "Mary asked some family members how many glasses of water they drink daily. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Glasses of Water</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHow many family members did Mary consult?"] [:db/cas 17592186147308 :question/problem "The following frequency table shows the number of home runs scored by the players of a baseball team during some playoffs. How many players batted between $4$ and $9$ home runs?\n\n| Home Runs | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-3$ | $3$ |\n| $4-6$ | $2$ |\n| $7-9$ | $4$ |\n| $10-12$ | $1$ |" "The following frequency table shows the number of home runs scored by the players of a baseball team during some playoffs. How many players batted between $4$ and $9$ home runs?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Home Runs</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186147308 :question/worked-solution "| Home Runs | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-3$ | $3$ |\n| $4-6$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |\n| $7-9$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n| $10-12$ | $1$ |\n\nFrom the frequency table:\n\n- $\\color{blue}2$ players batted between $4-6$ home runs\n- $\\color{blue}4$ players batted between $7-9$ home runs\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} +{\\color{blue}4} =6\n$$\n\nplayers batted between $4$ and $9$ home runs." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Home Runs</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#FDEDEC\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4-6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#FDEDEC\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7-9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the frequency table:\n\n- $\\color{blue}2$ players batted between $4-6$ home runs\n- $\\color{blue}4$ players batted between $7-9$ home runs\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} +{\\color{blue}4} =6\n$$\n\nplayers batted between $4$ and $9$ home runs."] [:db/cas 17592186147318 :question/problem "A certain data set has the features listed in the table below.\n\n| **Smallest Value** | **Lower Quartile** | **Median** | **Upper Quartile** | **Greatest Value** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $9$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nDraw a box plot for this data." "A certain data set has the features listed in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Smallest Value</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Lower Quartile</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Median</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Upper Quartile</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Greatest Value</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nDraw a box plot for this data."] [:db/cas 17592186147321 :question/problem "The frequency table below gives the weights, measured to the nearest kilogram, of some students in a statistics class.\n\n| Weight (kg) | Number of Students |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $50{-}59$ | $6$ |\n| $60{-}69$ | $10$ |\n| $70{-}79$ | $8$ |\n| $80{-}89$ | $2$ |\n\nDraw a histogram that represents the same data set." "The frequency table below gives the weights, measured to the nearest kilogram, of some students in a statistics class.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Weight (kg)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Students</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50{-}59$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60{-}69$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$70{-}79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80{-}89$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nDraw a histogram that represents the same data set."] [:db/cas 17592186147382 :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ y(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<       0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0, \\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,0).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) <       0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/69/6940ca72733614d6916831fc0316ae75ca0bb07f75082c36be3897ebf9fd55d5.png)" "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ y(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<       0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-2,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$y+2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0, \\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,0).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) <       0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/69/6940ca72733614d6916831fc0316ae75ca0bb07f75082c36be3897ebf9fd55d5.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186147385 :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/42/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"." "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(-\\infty,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(0,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$y^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$y-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}-}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}+}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/42/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"."] [:db/cas 17592186147388 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2, \\infty).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering $y'= y^2 -4.$ \nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-2,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2,\\infty).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider $y'=4-y^2.$\nFirst, $f(y)=4-y^2,$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\nHowever, we note that $f(y) <     0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider $y'=(y-2)^2.$ In this case, $f(y)= (y-2)^2,$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=2$ only.  Therefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2, \\infty).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the